t now what it can do  though is because it only uses a very  small amount of sample and so in the  example I've shown today you can imagine  sort of combining the two different  scenarios so let's say you take 5  microliters dilute it to a total of 50  but then you do a 2 microliter assay so  without accounting for any slopper or  excess you might need for pipetting you  know in theory you can run 25 replicate  wells so let's say you know to be safe  you could run 20 replicate wells with  that single 5 micro liter of diluted  sample so you can actually perform those  assays in parallel and because we are  offering a 384-well format you can  imagine you can fit a lot of well on 384  well plate so it's not true multiplexing  in the exact same well but it's  important to note that when bead base  multiplex assays are made like a Luminex  platform based assay sometimes  compromises have to be made on which  antibodies to use because you also have  to make sure that there's no cross  reactivity because you're trying to  measure each of the analytes all  together in one well well sometimes you  may have to pass on certain antibodies  that could give greater sensitivity and  the course in in the case of per quantum  we don't have to worry about that we're  able to settle on the best antibody that  not only gives us the great performance  in terms of sensitivity but also meets  the strict criteria for spike recovery  parallelism etc so definitely keeping  the DMM detection separate can sometimes  be beneficial and so there is a way to  do parallel parallel measurements  there's no washed-up cause higher  background increased nonspecific binding  you know that's a natural question a lot  of people see that cartoon that I show  with the the two antibodies and the hour  goes and free solution and people just  naturally assume that if the Y base is  present with those two antibodies and  free solutions you're just going to  naturally ligate those two all of those  together even